6. THE DENSITY TARGET ITSELF: what a perfect map could score
==============================================================================
 sigma sum - count, reflect  sum - count, zeros  mean, zeros  recall (val)  precision (val)  F1 (val)
   1.0              0.000%              1.640%       0.456%         0.952            1.000     0.975
   1.5              0.000%              2.317%       0.713%         0.933            1.000     0.965
   2.0              0.000%              2.869%       0.959%         0.853            1.000     0.921
   2.5              0.000%              3.349%       1.203%         0.798            1.000     0.888
   3.0              0.000%              3.775%       1.448%         0.749            1.000     0.857
   4.0              0.000%              4.503%       1.942%         0.615            1.000     0.761
sigma 1.0 has the best peak F1 on validation; on test the same map reaches recall 0.950, precision 1.000, F1 0.974. That is the CEILING of peak counting, set by the 451 pairs of centres closer than a pixel apart and by how wide the bumps are - not by the network.
The two sum columns answer the notebook's 'check that your target sums to the count': scipy's gaussian_filter
reflects at the border by default, which folds the truncated tail back in, so the map sums to the
count to 3 decimals. Cutting the kernel off instead (mode='constant', what a convolution layer with
zero padding does) loses up to the printed percentage for the worst of the 441 border centres - the
training script therefore checks the sum and rescales if it ever matters.

